<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daodejing</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tao Te Ching</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Daodejing (Tao Te Ching) is the founding text of Daoism, a short collection of about 81 sections of verse traditionally attributed to the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a>. It teaches that the Dao, the nameless source of all things, works through non-action and yielding, and it advises rulers to govern lightly. The oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> versions are bamboo slips of about 300 BCE from Guodian in the State of Chu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> copies from Mawangdui.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daodejing.jpg" alt="Daodejing" width="320" height="706" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mawangdui_LaoTsu_Ms2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The text is traditionally ascribed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a>, whose historical existence is debated; his name means Old Master. The first biographical reference to him is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>. Tradition makes him an older contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, but no scholarship in the West follows an early dating of the whole text, and most modern scholars hold it to be a compilation. Sayings attributed to Laozi were probably written down from the second half of the 5th century BCE and reached a relatively stable form in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, around the middle of the 3rd century BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscript</a> finds have changed the debate. The Guodian bamboo slips of about 300 BCE are shorter than the received text and lack its last fifteen chapters. Two nearly complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> copies were found in 1973 in a tomb at Mawangdui dated to 168 BCE, and they put the Te Ching half before the Tao Ching.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The received text has about 5,162 to 5,450 Chinese characters in 81 brief chapters, in two parts: the Daojing, chapters 1 to 37, and the Dejing, chapters 38 to 81. The Heshang Gong version divided it into 81 chapters; earlier versions are reported with 64, 68 or 72. The style is laconic and poetic, combining short declarative statements with deliberate contradictions, and it names no particular persons, times or places.</p><p>The text describes the Dao as the source and ideal of all existence, unseen but not transcendent, immensely powerful yet humble. It aims to lead its students back to a natural state in harmony with the Dao, and it treats language and conventional wisdom as biased and artificial. A central concept is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-wei">wu wei</a>, literally non-action.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The three main transmitted editions are named after the commentaries of Yan Zun, Heshang Gong and Wang Bi. From the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> it was mainly transmitted with the last two, and in 719 Wang Bi's was declared authoritative, though Heshang Gong's remained dominant into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>.</p><p>It is foundational to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a> and has had a deep influence on Chinese culture, art and medicine. Its terms were reinterpreted by Legalist thinkers, Confucians and above all by Chinese Buddhism. First translated into French in the early 19th century, it is one of the most translated texts in world literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tao Te Ching” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The oldest known manuscript of parts of the <b>Daodejing</b> was found in a Chu tomb at Guodian, written on bamboo slips around 300 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Taoism has its own literature, from the <b>Daodejing</b>, the Liezi and the Zhuangzi to the Taoist Canon, while the folk sects and vernacular literature produced the baojuan, “precious scrolls”, and morality books with ledgers of merit and demerit.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Already the historian Sima Qian, writing around 100 BCE, was uncertain who Laozi had been, and modern scholars regard the <b>Daodejing</b> as a compilation of sayings that took shape over the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
